  "id": "Compound::DB00226 - Disease::DOID:10763",
  "verbalization": "Guanadrel's potential reutilization for treating hypertension is supported by its interaction with Slc6a2, an organic heterocyclic compound involved in the regulation of neurotransmitter transport. This interaction is further linked to the sympathetic nervous system, which is localized to hypertension, suggesting a possible role in the pathophysiology of the disease. The evidence also highlights that Guanadrel, through its binding to Slc6a2, may influence the expression of genes associated with the sympathetic nervous system, thereby contributing to the development or maintenance of hypertension. The lowest common ancestor of Guanadrel and Guanethidine, both antihypertensive agents, reinforces the notion of a pharmacological relationship between these compounds and their role in treating hypertension. The integration of these pathways suggests that Guanadrel may exert its effects through a mechanism involving the sympathetic nervous system, potentially offering a novel therapeutic approach for hypertension.",
